{"parentUuid":"954ee73b-38ee-4a27-9b01-66443baeed85","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"23a4b2cd-c9ec-4ee6-8399-f284a0fae7e5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01P8VM1qiJPJks7rgQwxqRUu","type":"tool_result","content":"79\tTo reduce over-redaction, Entire preserves structural transcript fields such as IDs and paths, leaves placeholder values alone, and redacts only credential values for bounded key/value forms. Placeholders are detected by exact match (e.g. `changeme`, `example`, `placeholder`, `your_password`, `your_secret`, prior `REDACTED`/`[REDACTED]`/`<REDACTED>` markers) or by shape: shell expansions like `${DB_PASSWORD}`, bracketed names like `<password>` or `<your-db-password>`, and mask runs of three or more `*`/`x`/`.`/`-` (so `***`, `xxxx`, `....`, `----` all match). When a connection string contains a real password, it is redacted as a unit because partial fragments can still expose sensitive material; connection strings whose passwords are placeholders are left intact.\n80\t\n81\t## Customizing redaction\n82\t\n83\tThe built-in detectors handle well-known secret formats. For anything else you don't want stored in transcripts — internal credential shapes the bundled scanners don't know about, project codenames, or specific words and phrases you'd rather keep out of session archives — Entire offers two extension surfaces. Both run plain regex matching against transcript content, so the rules can target any string pattern, not just credentials. Both feed the same engine and run as their own layer between connection-string detection and bounded credential KV detection.\n84\t\n85\t### Surface 1: Inline `redaction.custom_redactions`\n86\t\n87\tAdd a label → regex map under `redaction.custom_redactions` in `.entire/settings.json`:\n88\t\n89\t```json\n90\t{\n91\t  \"redaction\": {\n92\t    \"custom_redactions\": {\n93\t      \"acme_token\":  \"ACME_TOKEN_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}\",\n94\t      \"internal_id\": \"INTERNAL_[a-z]{6}_[0-9]{4}\"\n95\t    }\n96\t  }\n97\t}\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\t- The label is for diagnostics only; matches are replaced with the bare `REDACTED` token (matching the built-in secret layers, not the `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` token used for PII).\n101\t- Regexes follow [Go's RE2 syntax](https://pkg.go.dev/regexp/syntax). No lookarounds, no backreferences.\n102\t- A failed compile is logged once at startup and the rule is skipped — it will never crash the redactor.\n103\t- Override in `.entire/settings.local.json` for personal additions; entries merge per-key (override replaces the same key, leaves other keys intact)."}]},"uuid":"7eeeb405-e899-4e63-843f-e377f3064143","timestamp":"2026-05-12T12:17:13.137Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments/docs/security-and-privacy.md","content":"To reduce over-redaction, Entire preserves structural transcript fields such as IDs and paths, leaves placeholder values alone, and redacts only credential values for bounded key/value forms. Placeholders are detected by exact match (e.g. `changeme`, `example`, `placeholder`, `your_password`, `your_secret`, prior `REDACTED`/`[REDACTED]`/`<REDACTED>` markers) or by shape: shell expansions like `${DB_PASSWORD}`, bracketed names like `<password>` or `<your-db-password>`, and mask runs of three or more `*`/`x`/`.`/`-` (so `***`, `xxxx`, `....`, `----` all match). When a connection string contains a real password, it is redacted as a unit because partial fragments can still expose sensitive material; connection strings whose passwords are placeholders are left intact.\n\n## Customizing redaction\n\nThe built-in detectors handle well-known secret formats. For anything else you don't want stored in transcripts — internal credential shapes the bundled scanners don't know about, project codenames, or specific words and phrases you'd rather keep out of session archives — Entire offers two extension surfaces. Both run plain regex matching against transcript content, so the rules can target any string pattern, not just credentials. Both feed the same engine and run as their own layer between connection-string detection and bounded credential KV detection.\n\n### Surface 1: Inline `redaction.custom_redactions`\n\nAdd a label → regex map under `redaction.custom_redactions` in `.entire/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"redaction\": {\n    \"custom_redactions\": {\n      \"acme_token\":  \"ACME_TOKEN_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}\",\n      \"internal_id\": \"INTERNAL_[a-z]{6}_[0-9]{4}\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- The label is for diagnostics only; matches are replaced with the bare `REDACTED` token (matching the built-in secret layers, not the `[REDACTED_<LABEL>]` token used for PII).\n- Regexes follow [Go's RE2 syntax](https://pkg.go.dev/regexp/syntax). No lookarounds, no backreferences.\n- A failed compile is logged once at startup and the rule is skipped — it will never crash the redactor.\n- Override in `.entire/settings.local.json` for personal additions; entries merge per-key (override replaces the same key, leaves other keys intact).","numLines":25,"startLine":79,"totalLines":231}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"954ee73b-38ee-4a27-9b01-66443baeed85","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments","sessionId":"13e26df4-5885-4c44-9469-8449e56488e6","version":"2.1.129","gitBranch":"feat/redaction-rule-extensions"}
